ext{for}\\,n\\ge 3.\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(3).$ To do this, we substitute $n=3$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that the left-hand side is less than the right-hand side:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=3.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(3+1)=24,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{3}=27,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=3.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 3.$ \n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 3,$ we have\n\n$$\n6(k+1)<{k}^{3}.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n6((k+1)+1)=6k+2<(k+1)^{3}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is less than the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6k+2 & =6(k+1)+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{6(k+1)}_{⏟}}_{<{k}^{3}}+6 & <{k}^{3}+6 \\\\  & ={k}^{3}+{{5}_{⏟}}_{<3{k}^{2}+3k}+1 \\\\  & <{k}^{3}+3{k}^{2}+3k+1 \\\\  & =(k+1)^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ is less than the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $6(n+1)<{n}^{3}$ for all $n\\ge 3.$" 13194139534322 17592186045417 true] [17592186413945 1071 "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$(n+2)^{2}\\le {3}^{n+1}$ for all $n\\ge 1$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements." 